{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQpmHv6WYQoqNoC23Kki","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Thanks. That answers Q1 and Q2. Refunds were waiting on Q1, so that question is below.\n\n**Settled**\n- **The discount is a percentage on the whole order.** Your request said \"a discount field to orders\", so this is one `discountPercent` on the order, not one per line. Tell me if you meant per-line.\n- **VAT is charged on the discounted amount.** I read \"applied before VAT\" as: take the discount off first, then work out VAT on what's left. That was option (a) in my Q2. My \"Before\" label in that question meant the opposite, so please confirm I've read you right. Example: 3000 cents subtotal with 10% off gives 2700 net, 540 VAT, 3240 total.\n- Everything from the previous round still holds: integer cents, 20% VAT rounded with `Math.round`, invoices that reconcile, and the refund quantity limit.\n\n**Mine (tell me if you disagree with any)**\n- **Field:** `order.discountPercent` is a number from 0 to 100, decimals allowed (e.g. 12.5). If it's missing it counts as 0, so existing orders and tests behave exactly as now. Anything outside 0–100 throws an error.\n- **Discount amount:** `discountCents = Math.round(subtotal × pct / 100)`. VAT is then `Math.round((subtotal − discountCents) × 0.2)`.\n- **Invoice:** shows `subtotalCents`, `discountCents`, `taxCents`, and `totalCents = subtotal − discount + tax`. I'll add a test that checks the totals reconcile when there is a discount.\n- **Rounding:** refunds are rounded per line, as they already are today. That means refunding every line one by one can come to a cent more or less than the order total. This is already true of VAT now; I'm keeping the existing behaviour, not changing it.\n\n**Question**\n\n1. **How much does a customer get back when they refund a line from a discounted order?** Right now `refundLine` returns the full line price plus VAT on it.\n   - (a) **They get back what they actually paid for that line.** The order's percentage comes off the line price, then VAT is added on the reduced amount. With 10% off, refunding one unit at 1000 cents gives 900 + 180 = 1080.\n   - (b) **They get back the full line price plus VAT** (1200), the same as now. Customers are refunded more than they paid, and the shop loses the discount on every return.\n   - *Recommendation:* (a). The refund matches the payment, and the VAT refunded matches the VAT that was charged, which keeps your VAT records consistent.\n\nOnce you answer this, I'll build and verify the change. Because it touches money, the final report will flag it for human review."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1463,"cache_read_input_tokens":39170,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1463},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6d660816-a45c-4fe6-93b0-f2db75291d78","uuid":"fd09e147-d115-452c-80ee-61c6fd4905c4","timestamp":"2026-11-26T03:24:43.771Z","request_id":"req_011ChQpmHmQKwsP4SBUyVSVv"}
